text:
• How the District’s data will be used, consistent with IC 33-133;
• How any user data collected will be analyzed, shared, or used and whether the provider can
  ensure the data is not used for AI model training;
• What kinds of data the provider keeps, how long it is kept, and who will have access to it;
• Measures they take to protect student and staff data; and
• Whether the tool complies with the requirements of the Children’s Online Privacy Protection Act
  (COPPA), the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (REPA), the Protection of Pupil Rights
  Amendment (PPRA), the Children’s Internet Protection Act (CIPA), the Individuals with
  Disabilities Education Act (IDEA), Title VI, Idaho student data privacy laws, and any Idaho AI
  legislation.

[graphic] Data security/ privacy. L1 (Pilot): District accounts/ settings; no-student-PII rule;
time-bound pilot. L2 (Approved): Published workflows + enforcement; required verification routine.
L3 (Integrated): Periodic audits; vendor-change monitoring. L4 (Mature): Regular risk reviews;
ongoing training updates.

Any AI tools or programs utilized by the District must adhere to State and federal laws; State rules;
and Federal regulations governing data use, privacy, and security. Any AI tool used must comply
with the District’s policies on data privacy and security, especially concerning student information.

next_context:
• Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA);
• Education Law Section 2-d (Unauthorized Release of Personally Identifiable Information);
• Labor Law Section 203-d (Employee Personal Identifying Information);
• State Technology Law Section 208 (Notification of Security Breach of Private Information).
Further, if the information would not be disclosed in response to a Freedom of Information Law
(FOIL) request, it should not be entered into GenAI.

